(Practice passage: how memory consolidates) Tj
/F1 12 Tf
16 TL
0 -34 Td
(Spaced repetition is a learning technique in which reviews of material are) Tj
T*
(scheduled at increasing intervals. Each successful recall strengthens the) Tj
T*
(memory trace and lengthens the time before it decays, so the next review can) Tj
T*
(be pushed further into the future.) Tj
T*
T*
(The forgetting curve, first described by Hermann Ebbinghaus in 1885, shows) Tj
T*
(that retention drops steeply within hours of learning and then levels off.) Tj
T*
(Reviewing just before a memory would otherwise be lost produces a larger gain) Tj
T*
(than reviewing material that is still fresh.) Tj
T*
T*
(Active recall amplifies the effect. Retrieving an answer from memory, rather) Tj
T*
(than rereading it, forces the brain to reconstruct the knowledge. That) Tj
T*
(effortful reconstruction is what makes the memory durable. Flashcards combine) Tj
T*
(both ideas: they prompt recall, and a scheduler decides when each card should) Tj
T*
(return.) Tj
T*
T*
(Try it: select this paragraph and tap Flashcards.) Tj
T*
